      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "The majority of patients with advanced malignant disease experience pain, so pain is commonly present in patients with paraneoplastic syndromes. It is rare, however, that the pain itself is a paraneoplastic manifestation of cancer. Usually, the pain in this context is associated with a paraneoplastic syndrome but is not a direct result of that syndrome. Three syndromes in which pain is part of the syndrome and a paraneoplastic manifestation of malignant disease--neuropathy, ganglionitis, and monolitis--have been described in the literature. These syndromes and their management are discussed in this article."},
